# =============================================================================
# Lv-00 Axiom Package: Elliptic Geometry (Single Elliptic Plane)
# =============================================================================
#
# Corresponding Mathematical Theory:
#   Elliptic geometry (single elliptic geometry), a non-Euclidean geometry
#   in which Euclid's parallel postulate does not hold. Unlike Euclidean
#   geometry (exactly one parallel) and hyperbolic geometry (infinitely
#   many parallels), elliptic geometry has NO parallel lines: any two
#   distinct lines in the plane must intersect at exactly one point.
#
#   The single elliptic plane is obtained from the sphere S^2 by identifying
#   antipodal points {P, -P} to a single elliptic point. Elliptic lines
#   correspond to great circles reduced by this identification. Since any
#   two great circles on S^2 intersect at a pair of antipodal points, and
#   these pairs are identified, any two elliptic lines intersect at exactly
#   one point.
#
#   The elliptic plane is equivalent to the real projective plane RP^2
#   equipped with a metric (Cayley-Klein metric).
#
# Axiomatization Method:
#   Based on Hilbert's axiomatic framework (Grundlagen der Geometrie, 1899),
#   with the following modifications for elliptic geometry:
#     - Group I (Incidence): Modified — any two distinct lines intersect.
#       The uniqueness of the line through two points is preserved.
#     - Group II (Betweenness): REPLACED by Separation/Cyclic Order axioms.
#       Betweenness is not well-defined on elliptic lines (which are
#       topologically circles), so it is replaced by the concept of
#       separation of point pairs on a line.
#     - Group III (Congruence): Modified — segment congruence is bounded
#       (no segment can exceed half the circumference). Angle congruence
#       and SAS criterion are preserved.
#     - Group IV (Parallel Postulate): REPLACED by the Elliptic Parallel
#       Axiom — no parallel lines exist; any two lines intersect.
#     - Group V (Continuity): Modified — Archimedes' axiom holds but
#       line completeness is adapted for the finite total length of lines.
#
# References:
#   - Wikipedia: Elliptic geometry
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_geometry
#   - Coxeter, H.S.M. "Introduction to Geometry" (2nd ed., 1969), Wiley.
#     Chapters 6-7 (Non-Euclidean Geometry).
#   - Coxeter, H.S.M. "Non-Euclidean Geometry" (6th ed., 1998), MAA.
#   - Greenberg, M.J. (2007). "Euclidean and Non-Euclidean Geometries"
#     (4th ed.). W.H. Freeman. Chapter 7.
#   - Meserve, B.E. (1983). "Fundamental Concepts of Geometry."
#     Dover. Chapter 5.
#   - Wikipedia: Spherical geometry
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spherical_geometry
#   - Wikipedia: Real projective plane
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_projective_plane
#   - Wikipedia: Cayley-Klein metric
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley%E2%80%93Klein_metric
#   - Sommerville, D.M.Y. (1914). "The Elements of Non-Euclidean Geometry."
#     Bell. Chapter IV.
#   - Bonola, R. (1955). "Non-Euclidean Geometry." Dover.
#     (Historical treatment of elliptic and hyperbolic geometry.)
#
# Primitive Notions (4 total):
#   Terms: point, line (elliptic line = great circle with antipodal ID)
#   Relations: incidence (lies_on), separation (cyclic order on lines),
#              segment_congruence, angle_congruence
#
# Key Properties Distinguishing Elliptic from Euclidean/Hyperbolic:
#   1. NO parallel lines: any two distinct lines intersect at exactly one point.
#   2. Lines are finite and closed (topologically circles).
#   3. The total length of any line is bounded (pi * R in the spherical model).
#   4. Triangle angle sum > 180 degrees (excess, not defect).
#   5. The Pythagorean theorem does NOT hold.
#   6. Similarity implies congruence (no scaling).
#   7. The space is compact, homogeneous, isotropic.
#   8. The space is non-orientable (for the 2D single elliptic plane).
#   9. Every point has a unique absolute polar line (pole-polar duality).
#   10. Two lines perpendicular to a given line intersect at the absolute pole.
#
# Core Axioms (5 groups):
#   I.   Elliptic Incidence (4 axioms) — modified from Hilbert
#   II.  Separation / Cyclic Order (4 axioms) — replaces Betweenness
#   III. Congruence (6 axioms) — modified for bounded segments
#   IV.  Elliptic Parallel Postulate (1 axiom) — replaces Euclid V
#   V.   Continuity (2 axioms) — modified for finite line length
#
# Constraint Templates (30 total, in 8 groups):
#   Group I:   Elliptic Incidence Axioms     (4 templates)
#   Group II:  Separation / Cyclic Order      (4 templates)
#   Group III: Congruence Axioms              (6 templates)
#   Group IV:  Elliptic Parallel Postulate    (2 templates)
#   Group V:   Elliptic-Specific Properties   (6 templates)
#   Group VI:  Continuity & Metric            (3 templates)
#   Group VII: Model Constructions            (3 templates)
#   Group VIII: Derived Constructors          (2 templates)
#
# Known Unconstructible Problems (6):
#   1. squaring_the_circle_elliptic
#   2. angle_trisection_elliptic
#   3. doubling_the_cube_elliptic
#   4. regular_heptagon_elliptic
#   5. constructible_length_characterization
#   6. triangle_similarity_without_congruence
#
# Author: Lv-00 Axiom Package System
# Date: 2026-05-23
# Version: 1.0.0
# =============================================================================

axiom "elliptic_geometry" "1.0.0" {

    # =================================================================
    # Constraint Templates (Legal Constructors & Metrics)
    # =================================================================
    # Each template represents a fundamental geometric operation or
    # relation in elliptic plane geometry. Parameters are geometric
    # objects (points, lines, segments, angles).
    #
    # Groups I-III and VI share structural similarities with Euclidean
    # and hyperbolic geometry, but have key modifications for the
    # elliptic setting (finite lines, no parallels, cyclic order).
    # Groups IV-V and VII-VIII are specific to elliptic geometry.
    # =================================================================

    # --- Group I: Elliptic Incidence Axioms (4) ---
    # Modified from Hilbert's incidence axioms for the elliptic plane.
    # Key change: any two distinct lines intersect at exactly one point.

    # E-I1: For every two distinct points, there exists exactly one line
    #     containing both. (Same as Euclidean I1.)
    # Params: point A, point B -> line AB
    template "line_through_two_points" 2

    # E-I2: Every line contains at least two distinct points.
    #     (Same as Euclidean I2.)
    # Params: line l -> (exists point A, point B on l)
    template "line_has_two_points" 1

    # E-I3: There exist at least three non-collinear points.
    #     (Same as Euclidean I3.)
    # Params: (zero-input existence axiom)
    template "existence_of_triangle" 0

    # E-I4: Any two distinct lines intersect at exactly one point.
    #     This is the ELLIPTIC INCIDENCE AXIOM — the defining property
    #     that distinguishes elliptic from Euclidean and hyperbolic
    #     geometry. There are NO parallel lines in elliptic geometry.
    # Params: line l1, line l2 -> intersection point P
    template "any_two_lines_intersect" 2

    # --- Group II: Separation / Cyclic Order Axioms (4) ---
    # REPLACES Hilbert's Betweenness axioms. On an elliptic line
    # (topologically a circle), betweenness is not globally well-defined.
    # Instead, we use the concept of SEPARATION: a pair of points {A,B}
    # SEPARATES a pair {C,D} on a line if C and D lie in different
    # arcs of the line determined by A and B.

    # S1: Existence of separation: For any four distinct collinear points,
    #     exactly one of the three possible pairings separates the other.
    #     Given points A, B, C, D on line l, either {A,B}|{C,D},
    #     {A,C}|{B,D}, or {A,D}|{B,C} is the separation relation.
    # Params: point A, point B, point C, point D -> separation relation
    template "separation_relation" 4

    # S2: Symmetry of separation: If {A,B} separates {C,D}, then
    #     {C,D} separates {A,B}. Separation is a mutual relation.
    # Params: point A, point B, point C, point D
    template "separation_symmetry" 4

    # S3: Transitivity of separation: If {A,B} separates {C,D} and
    #     {A,C} separates {D,E}, then {A,D} separates {B,E}.
    #     This ensures the cyclic order structure is consistent.
    # Params: point A, point B, point C, point D, point E
    template "separation_transitivity" 5

    # S4: Richness of separation: Given three distinct collinear points
    #     A, B, C, there exists a point D such that {A,B} separates {C,D}.
    #     This replaces the Euclidean extension axiom (B2) — on a circle,
    #     we can always find a point on the "other side."
    # Params: point A, point B, point C -> point D
    template "separation_extension" 3

    # --- Group III: Congruence Axioms (6) ---
    # Modified from Hilbert's congruence axioms. Segment congruence is
    # bounded: no segment can exceed half the total line length (pi*R).
    # Angle congruence and SAS are preserved.

    # C1: Bounded segment transport: Given segment AB and point A' on
    #     line l, there exists B' on l such that AB = A'B', PROVIDED
    #     that |AB| <= pi*R (half the circumference). Segment transport
    #     fails for distances exceeding half the line length.
    # Params: point A, point B, point A_prime, line l -> point B_prime
    template "bounded_segment_transport" 4

    # C2: Segment congruence is reflexive: AB = AB.
    #     (Same as Euclidean C2.)
    # Params: point A, point B
    template "segment_congruence_reflexive" 2

    # C3: Segment congruence is transitive and symmetric:
    #     AB = CD and CD = EF implies AB = EF.
    #     (Same as Euclidean C3.)
    # Params: segment AB, segment CD, segment EF (6 points)
    template "segment_congruence_transitive" 6

    # C4: Angle transport: Given angle BAC and ray A'B', there exists
    #     ray A'C' on a given side such that angle BAC = angle B'A'C'.
    #     (Same as Euclidean C4 — angle transport is unbounded.)
    # Params: angle vertex A, rays AB,AC; new vertex A', ray A'B', side
    template "angle_transport" 5

    # C5: Angle congruence properties (reflexive, symmetric, transitive).
    #     (Same as Euclidean C5.)
    # Params: angle ABC (3 points), angle DEF (3 points)
    template "angle_congruence_properties" 6

    # C6: SAS (Side-Angle-Side) congruence criterion:
    #     If AB = A'B', AC = A'C', and angle BAC = angle B'A'C',
    #     then triangle ABC = triangle A'B'C'.
    #     (Same as Euclidean C6 — SAS holds in elliptic geometry.)
    # Params: 6 points (A,B,C and A',B',C')
    template "SAS_congruence" 6

    # --- Group IV: Elliptic Parallel Postulate (2) ---
    # REPLACES Euclid's parallel postulate. In elliptic geometry,
    # there are NO parallel lines. This is the defining axiom.

    # E-IV: Elliptic Parallel Postulate — there are no parallel lines.
    #     Given any line l and any point P (whether on l or not on l),
    #     every line through P intersects l. In other words, for any
    #     two distinct lines l1, l2, there exists a unique point
    #     P = l1 ∩ l2.
    # Params: line l, point P -> all lines through P intersect l
    template "no_parallel_lines" 2

    # Corollary: The elliptic plane satisfies the projective incidence
    #     property — the space of lines through any point covers the
    #     entire plane. Every line through P intersects every other line.
    # Params: point P -> pencil of all lines through P covers plane
    template "projective_incidence_property" 1

    # --- Group V: Elliptic-Specific Properties (6) ---

    # Pole-polar duality: Every point P has a unique absolute polar line
    #     p, and every line l has a unique absolute pole L. Point P lies
    #     on line l if and only if pole L lies on polar p. The distance
    #     from a point to its polar is a quadrant (pi/2 * R).
    # Params: point P -> polar line p
    template "absolute_polar_line" 1

    # Absolute pole: Every line l has a unique absolute pole L such that
    #     L is the intersection of all lines perpendicular to l.
    #     Two lines perpendicular to l intersect at the pole of l.
    # Params: line l -> pole point L
    template "absolute_pole" 1

    # Elliptic distance: The distance between two points P and Q on the
    #     elliptic plane is proportional to the angle between their
    #     absolute polars. In the spherical model of radius R:
    #     d(P,Q) = R * arccos(P . Q), where 0 <= d(P,Q) <= pi*R/2.
    #     The maximum distance is pi*R/2 (half the circumference).
    # Params: point P, point Q -> elliptic distance d(P,Q)
    template "elliptic_distance" 2

    # Triangle angle excess: In elliptic geometry, the sum of interior
    #     angles of any triangle is STRICTLY GREATER than pi (180 degrees).
    #     The excess E = (sum of angles) - pi is proportional to the
    #     area of the triangle: Area = R^2 * E (Girard's theorem for
    #     the sphere, adapted to the elliptic plane).
    # Params: triangle vertices A, B, C -> angle excess E
    template "triangle_angle_excess" 3

    # Polar triangle: Given triangle ABC, its polar triangle A'B'C' is
    #     formed by the poles of the sides of ABC. The vertices of the
    #     polar triangle are the poles of the lines BC, CA, AB. The
    #     polar triangle has the property that its angles are the
    #     supplements of the sides of the original triangle, and vice
    #     versa (polar duality).
    # Params: triangle vertices A, B, C -> polar triangle A', B', C'
    template "polar_triangle" 3

    # Similarity implies congruence: In elliptic geometry, if two
    #     triangles have corresponding angles equal, then they are
    #     congruent (corresponding sides are also equal). There is NO
    #     notion of similar-but-non-congruent figures. This is because
    #     the space is compact — scaling is impossible.
    # Params: triangle ABC, triangle DEF (6 points) -> congruence
    template "similarity_implies_congruence" 6

    # --- Group VI: Continuity & Metric (3) ---

    # V.1 (Archimedes' Axiom, bounded): Given segments AB and CD with
    #     |CD| > 0, there exists a natural number n such that n copies
    #     of CD laid end-to-end on the line through A and B will
    #     "wrap around" the elliptic line. Since elliptic lines are
    #     finite circles, Archimedes' axiom takes a cyclic form.
    # Params: segment AB, segment CD (4 points)
    template "elliptic_archimedes_axiom" 4

    # V.2 (Completeness, adapted): The points of an elliptic line form
    #     a complete metric space isometric to the circle S^1 / {+/-}
    #     (circle with antipodal identification). The coordinate model
    #     is [0, pi) with wrap-around identification.
    # Params: (zero-input, structural axiom)
    template "elliptic_line_completeness" 0

    # Elliptic area: The total area of the elliptic plane is finite.
    #     In the spherical model of radius R, the total area is
    #     2*pi*R^2 (half the sphere's surface area, since antipodal
    #     points are identified). The area of a triangle with angle
    #     excess E is R^2 * E (Girard's theorem, adapted).
    # Params: triangle vertices A, B, C -> area
    template "elliptic_area" 3

    # --- Group VII: Model Constructions (3) ---

    # Spherical model: The elliptic plane is modeled as the unit sphere
    #     S^2 with antipodal points identified. Points are pairs {P, -P}
    #     on S^2. Lines are great circles with antipodal identification.
    #     The distance between {P,-P} and {Q,-Q} is the lesser of the
    #     two spherical distances d(P,Q) and d(P,-Q).
    # Params: sphere radius R -> spherical model of elliptic plane
    template "spherical_model" 1

    # Projective model (Cayley-Klein): The elliptic plane is modeled as
    #     the real projective plane RP^2 with the Cayley-Klein metric.
    #     Points are lines through the origin in R^3. The distance
    #     between points [u] and [v] is:
    #     d([u],[v]) = arccos(|u . v| / (||u|| ||v||)).
    #     Lines are planes through the origin in R^3.
    # Params: coordinate dimension (3) -> projective model
    template "projective_model" 1

    # Gnomonic projection: Maps the hemisphere to a tangent plane.
    #     Great circles on the sphere map to straight lines in the
    #     plane. This provides a connection between the spherical model
    #     and the projective model. The projection covers exactly half
    #     the elliptic plane (one hemisphere).
    # Params: hemisphere center, tangent plane -> gnomonic projection map
    template "gnomonic_projection" 2

    # --- Group VIII: Derived Constructors (2) ---

    # Perpendicular from a point to a line: Given a point P and a line l,
    #     there exists a unique line through P perpendicular to l.
    #     In elliptic geometry, the foot of the perpendicular is the
    #     point on l closest to P (minimum distance).
    # Params: point P, line l -> perpendicular line, foot of perpendicular
    template "perpendicular_from_point" 2

    # Elliptic midpoint: Given two distinct points A and B on an
    #     elliptic line, there exist exactly TWO midpoints M1 and M2
    #     (unlike Euclidean geometry which has exactly one). The two
    #     midpoints are antipodal to each other. This is because the
    #     line is a circle: going halfway from A to B in either
    #     direction gives a midpoint.
    # Params: point A, point B -> midpoint pair M1, M2
    template "elliptic_midpoint_pair" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Known Unconstructible / Undecidable Problems
    # =================================================================

    # 1. Squaring the circle in elliptic geometry: constructing a square
    #    with the same area as a given circle. The area of an elliptic
    #    circle involves transcendental functions of the radius, making
    #    this impossible with geometric constructions.
    unconstructible "squaring_the_circle_elliptic" {
        reduces_to "transcendental_number"
        dependency "elliptic_distance"
        dependency "elliptic_area"
        dependency "elliptic_line_completeness"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squaring_the_circle"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 2. Angle trisection in elliptic geometry: dividing an arbitrary
    #    angle into three equal parts. This reduces to solving a general
    #    cubic equation, which is impossible with straightedge and compass
    #    constructions in any geometry (the algebraic obstructions are
    #    the same regardless of the ambient geometry).
    unconstructible "angle_trisection_elliptic" {
        reduces_to "cubic_equation_solving"
        dependency "angle_transport"
        dependency "SAS_congruence"
        dependency "bounded_segment_transport"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angle_trisection"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 3. Doubling the cube in elliptic geometry: constructing a segment
    #    whose length is the cube root of 2 times a given segment.
    #    This requires constructing the cube root of 2, which is
    #    algebraic of degree 3 and impossible with straightedge and
    #    compass regardless of the ambient geometry.
    unconstructible "doubling_the_cube_elliptic" {
        reduces_to "cube_root_of_two"
        dependency "bounded_segment_transport"
        dependency "elliptic_distance"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doubling_the_cube"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 4. Regular heptagon construction in elliptic geometry: constructing
    #    a regular 7-gon. The cosine of 2*pi/7 satisfies a cubic equation
    #    with Galois group S_3, making it non-constructible. The algebraic
    #    obstructions are the same as in Euclidean geometry.
    unconstructible "regular_heptagon_elliptic" {
        reduces_to "cubic_equation_solving"
        dependency "bounded_segment_transport"
        dependency "angle_transport"
        dependency "elliptic_distance"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constructible_polygon"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 5. Constructible length characterization in elliptic geometry:
    #    characterizing which lengths are constructible on an elliptic
    #    line. The constructible lengths are bounded by pi*R/2 and
    #    satisfy the same algebraic constraints as in Euclidean geometry
    #    (nested square roots), but the boundedness adds additional
    #    complexity to the characterization.
    unconstructible "constructible_length_characterization" {
        reduces_to "algebraic_number_theory"
        dependency "elliptic_distance"
        dependency "elliptic_line_completeness"
        dependency "bounded_segment_transport"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constructible_number"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 6. Triangle similarity without congruence: In elliptic geometry,
    #    any two triangles with equal corresponding angles are necessarily
    #    congruent (AAA is a congruence criterion). There do NOT exist
    #    similar-but-non-congruent triangles. This is a fundamental
    #    impossibility resulting from the compactness of the space.
    unconstructible "triangle_similarity_without_congruence" {
        reduces_to "compactness_of_elliptic_space"
        dependency "similarity_implies_congruence"
        dependency "SAS_congruence"
        dependency "triangle_angle_excess"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_geometry"
        green_verified true
    }

    # =================================================================
    # Bottom Geometry & Logical Framework
    # =================================================================

    # The bottom geometry is the single elliptic plane, modeled as
    # the real projective plane RP^2 with a Cayley-Klein metric.
    # Contradiction (bottom) is geometrically represented as the
    # degenerate case where the angle excess of a triangle would
    # need to exceed the maximum possible value.
    bottom_geometry "elliptic_plane_RP2"

    # Classical negation encoding: ¬φ is encoded as φ → ⊥, using
    # material implication. Elliptic geometry is a classical theory
    # (not constructive/intuitionistic).
    negation_encoding "classical_material_implication"

    # Explosion principle: ex contradictione quodlibet. From a
    # contradiction (⊥), any proposition follows. This is standard
    # for classical geometric theories.
    contradiction_behavior "explosion_principle"
}
